#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065076} :activity/id #uuid "0dc1fb7b-6336-57cd-b507-8bd6ddfc25fc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048189 :topic/id #uuid "86b8f7ea-5908-5137-a377-052e6fc4f731" :topic/math-academy-id 315} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065433 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral, apart from the coefficient of $4$ in the denominator, resembles the basic integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nIn fact, they look almost the same, but instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = (2x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can often use this trick whenever we see an integral that closely resembles an integral for an inverse trigonometric function.\n\n**Note:** As always, after solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53af5e98-4580-5243-a97c-de9e0d5f8fab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 863 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3e15f90-13b8-5437-b44e-fcdec07ad48c" :step/math-academy-id 641164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064218 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064090 :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342064}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"} :step/id #uuid "c5880b8e-1ebf-531e-9458-f3312a599e31" :step/math-academy-id 641165 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064091 :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428006}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "a6a53452-9624-525f-9b54-d025309e71da" :step/math-academy-id 641168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065124}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064220 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064092 :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428007}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "e5a617a2-9f5d-550f-a473-195c08b4798c" :step/math-academy-id 641174 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065182}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064101 :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342068}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"} :step/id #uuid "1c6cd5c4-2775-585d-9234-d995757ade98" :step/math-academy-id 641171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065182 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065381 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\text{arcsec}(ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "02539765-4345-5409-87f9-9901a59b7ebf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4800 :tutorial/title "Summary of Key Results"} :step/id #uuid "b420ebdd-a5d6-5ce1-8cde-23990b2968f7" :step/math-academy-id 641177}] :activity/title "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064411}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125368} :activity/id #uuid "ca8227e6-ef47-5702-8c4f-321ab31e8355" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729 :topic/id #uuid "b90e497c-df74-56b9-a5d7-98c7bdb922cb" :topic/math-academy-id 1920} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125899 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a straight line passing through the point $P(3,-1,7)$ and that's parallel to the direction vector ![](images/54/54405c5c6e2502e5174525c2ee36a99366a79933b47e316a09858f827fda8dae.svg) As we know, we can write the vector equation of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nUsing the vector equation above, we can create a set of **parametric equations** where we express a general point $(x,y,z)$ in terms of  the parameter $t.$\n\nIf we let ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) be the position vector of the general point $(x,y,z),$ then the above equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1+5t \\\\ 7-3t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n![](images/64/64cf325014eff341673557321ef565e35988b836630f3fbeb52af8024d7aaad7.svg)\n\nThe equations in the above system are called the **parametric equations** of the straight line.\nGeometrically, the equation states that a point belongs to the line if and only if its coordinates have the form\n\n$$\n(3, \\: -1+5t, \\: 7-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n![](images/e2/e29ce95cdccb47b97578f6431a96e608f7af9f6709410b221ff517b436896608.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ff73cc8-91a0-5a25-9c14-6e87496adac8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "de4015ef-1002-54f7-adee-e5eee4e87979" :step/math-academy-id 189149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124498 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124227 :question/id #uuid "001af492-25b1-5f75-b030-668708eee118" :question/math-academy-id "e-15602"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f232aed9-1584-5e57-a881-226a6876384a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400296}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Straight Line Given Parametrically"} :step/id #uuid "1c0d5ba6-1e60-5d4d-b42e-60ba32609db6" :step/math-academy-id 189150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124499 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124228 :question/id #uuid "7a6038c2-3f7b-5dfa-a9e2-256b246fcc20" :question/math-academy-id "e-15603"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3699391-68e8-5c59-8ca2-83fded9f22de" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398298}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Parallel To a Straight Line Given Parametrically"} :step/id #uuid "b5810a9c-9d11-54cc-9534-f5807fd71ff5" :step/math-academy-id 189153 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124500 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124229 :question/id #uuid "173b99e8-d00c-57c8-995e-ba198361480e" :question/math-academy-id "e-15604"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "debd4ccc-de40-55a6-9236-9bf9fff0bdb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302398}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Line"} :step/id #uuid "83088d79-842b-5477-8d05-56eb6c96e451" :step/math-academy-id 189156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124501 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124230 :question/id #uuid "a9c13555-9d30-5dfc-be5a-13f0597c2ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-15605"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d275a218-aa12-54bd-a4ff-4be892226f4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290485}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Segment"} :step/id #uuid "5a457aae-70d6-5725-8620-6593b8838a70" :step/math-academy-id 189159}] :activity/title "The Parametric Equations of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124717}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130036} :activity/id #uuid "b93dfde4-f3d0-56f7-90c3-7b5628613c8b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048800 :topic/id #uuid "c021e608-4c8b-5191-872f-f3e0b02d2d4a" :topic/math-academy-id 1983} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129173 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128992 :question/id #uuid "e17f982d-3d3e-5279-b507-d390e4f1970f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6116"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8537cc22-8d18-57c8-9ebf-921bd4d06ae7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048820}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366254}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Double Summation"} :step/id #uuid "ac75c745-5dbd-5824-9f7d-04a8faf6a86c" :step/math-academy-id 22705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130377 :tutorial/content "Recall that we use **sigma notation** to write sums concisely. For example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\n$$\n\nrefers to the sum of all terms in the sequence $a_i,$ where the **index** $i$ ranges from $1$ to $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i = a_1+a_2+\\cdots+a_n\n$$\n\nSigma notation can also represent sums whose terms depend on two indices. That is, **double sums** of the form \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $i$ ranges from $1$ to $m,$ and $j$ ranges from $1$ to $n\\mathbin.$ Thus, a double summation is just a \"sum of a sum.\" \n\nIn calculating double summations, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we evaluate the inner sum by fixing the index of the outer sum ($i$) and incrementing only the inner index ($j$).\n\n- Next, we evaluate the outer sum by incrementing the outer sum index.\n\nTo illustrate how it works, suppose we are given the following double sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\sum_{j=1}^4 (i+j)\n$$\n\n \n\nTo compute this double sum, we can proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{4}(i+j) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}[({\\sum }_{j=1}^{4}(i+j))] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}[(i+1)+(i+2)+(i+3)+(i+4)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}(4i+10) \\\\  & =4(1)+10+4(2)+10 \\\\  & =4+10+8+10 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLater, we will provide some formulas that will help us simplify many double summations calculations." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c842b98-cbdf-55a4-96a1-360697763430" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3867 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "583e493e-9206-508f-b782-7eec0883a7d5" :step/math-academy-id 22707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130378 :tutorial/content "Double summations satisfy the following properties:\n\n**The Constant Multiple Rule** \n\nWe can factor out a constant multiple from a double summation:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n k a_{ij} = k\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}\n$$\n\n**The Sum Rule** \n\nWe can distribute a double summation over a sum of terms.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (a_{ij} + b_{ij}) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} + \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n b_{ij}\n$$\n\n**The Double Sum of Units**\n\nRecall that for single summations, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n} 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\textrm{ times}}  = n.\n$$\n\nAnalogously for double summations, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n 1 = mn.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41df0fcf-551a-58a5-b47e-ac1ddd121f7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3868 :tutorial/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules"} :step/id #uuid "3d0a00d2-9ccc-5261-bfa0-15e1bf5cb3c6" :step/math-academy-id 22708 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129174 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128993 :question/id #uuid "e8f3608f-68f2-5ba9-b618-447887e7f6fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6118"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4312c28-e298-5185-9009-e6ca243175fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365122}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules"} :step/id #uuid "42e12d98-e4bf-512d-b3c2-5c93584fc740" :step/math-academy-id 22710 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130196}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128835 :question/id #uuid "d180f745-028b-5859-8cad-4a49b5c20773" :question/math-academy-id "e-10521"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08375770-c610-5dae-b01b-c457de086cba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371865}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule and Swap Rules"} :step/id #uuid "4e8c00d9-bc34-56d4-a71a-61e0e176e8fb" :step/math-academy-id 39727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130196 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130453 :tutorial/content "In addition, we have the following useful rules:\n\n**The Product Rule** \n\nWe can distribute a double summation over a product of *single-index* terms with *different* indices.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n  a_{i}b_j = \\sum_{i=1}^m a_i \\sum_{j=1}^n b_{j}\n$$\n\n**The Swap Rule** \n\nWe can swap the order of summations in a double summation.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}= \\sum_{j=1}^n\\sum_{i=1}^m a_{ij}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d25ceabe-e67a-59b3-9ed6-37be45644e5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6584 :tutorial/title "The Product and Swap Rules"} :step/id #uuid "5a28b905-cf2f-59e1-aaa2-6fb0064fcd4b" :step/math-academy-id 39728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130195}}] :activity/title "Double Summations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129331}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116133} :activity/id #uuid "d6f7c63b-f172-5766-b56a-db8bf2a22464" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048920 :topic/id #uuid "cc887b1d-8841-507d-a99b-b7888c08a675" :topic/math-academy-id 1728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116498 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n![](images/fd/fd9e9dc44e27810ef490a898a7c82ae9296eaf85ac9406cf310afe86638f71e1.svg) \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/e5/e553bcc265698432452c8d35b79fe9dc70c25010a0bcc6874ad0302ea7033ed7.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n![](images/b7/b7c08e9a28ebfe456a9d77ccdaf6688893dfd04ba755a989a417c8a43e80ee0c.svg)\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fc38ae42-2cd4-53df-9ad5-568d8e3b5fd4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3167 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d8efc5ed-eaaf-514d-b03a-248df406a7d5" :step/math-academy-id 17230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116136}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115078 :question/id #uuid "7c480d6a-93a1-56ec-a75c-56402e872c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-4723"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401995}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e874c19f-8e51-5ff3-b8d2-dd1a0540bbc2" :step/math-academy-id 17231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115079 :question/id #uuid "e13311fb-21de-58d4-aed0-18304b48bb0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4724"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417379}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"} :step/id #uuid "7cf9141d-1c90-5675-a333-b0aa4e0f3cef" :step/math-academy-id 17232 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115080 :question/id #uuid "ff4b4389-d8ff-5b48-904b-ff405abb4e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4725"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417378}] :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"} :step/id #uuid "e2dcd832-c069-5309-a7a3-27850adf28e2" :step/math-academy-id 17233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116135}}] :activity/title "Finding the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Using Row Operations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115474}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173004} :activity/id #uuid "81efeb5d-b89e-5c14-80c7-9be44b7d6319" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049012 :topic/id #uuid "d38bd816-27de-5026-97de-662b6ee04439" :topic/math-academy-id 3387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171898 :question/id #uuid "91f986c6-c305-50eb-a5b7-36043279d128" :question/math-academy-id "e-9505"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "035a4ded-7b92-5073-a8aa-3ffa47aff9ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259638}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "0a350174-3107-5bfc-b7cf-86f7ef4d3531" :step/math-academy-id 35594 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172093 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171899 :question/id #uuid "9779a982-2048-58e2-8a90-df2bcb366d96" :question/math-academy-id "e-9506"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd51cf5a-ca8a-5f89-91d6-8f1a58412314" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259657}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Representation of an Affine Transformation Given its Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "7d8f185d-3b89-591e-8ca2-fdba1ecfbeff" :step/math-academy-id 35595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172094 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171900 :question/id #uuid "2ddc4e15-3729-5425-953c-1cb95edc201b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9508"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6664fc2f-f776-5da1-823a-c5ab35873e51" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259644}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Representation of an Affine Transformation Given its Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "27929e1f-7191-5a61-bf7e-56af8100e9f0" :step/math-academy-id 35598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173002}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173206 :tutorial/content "An **affine transformation** $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function that can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nUnder this notation, \n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]$ represents vectors belonging to the input space, and\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$  represents vectors belonging to the output space.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis an affine transformation because it can be expressed in the form $\\mathbf x = A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+1 \\\\ 2u+v-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the transformation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis not affine since it contains the quadratic term $uv.$\n\nFinally, note the following relationships between affine and linear transformations:\n\n- A linear transformation $\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u$ is a special case of an affine transformation with $\\mathbf b = \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- For an affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ given by $\\mathbf x = A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$ we say that $A$ is *the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with* $\\mathbf T.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b154eb4-94cc-5d7b-8120-56085be85703" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5808 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d639fa1f-d747-5b31-9f98-2bdd4bf28119" :step/math-academy-id 35599 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173001}}] :activity/title "Affine Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172189}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162847} :activity/id #uuid "f83b8c51-727f-5eb0-9f6f-bcc3b133cfd6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/id #uuid "268943d4-aaa4-5e39-9db5-48d7523acdc3" :topic/math-academy-id 3118} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163222 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$A^T = A$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$\n\nOn the other hand, the matrix\n\n$$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fe9d8bc-6133-5ab4-8b4f-af2b40817e02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4880 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9c19c64f-0597-5273-8ff3-50729d7a67fc" :step/math-academy-id 30531 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161771 :question/id #uuid "c68d70a2-e4f8-5f08-8220-3df420a1e199" :question/math-academy-id "e-8156"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425154}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "f2c61bc7-19cc-5e6a-a533-0450e49aa5dd" :step/math-academy-id 30550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163254 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if $$A = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\qquad B = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$ then the product $A\\cdot B$ is $$\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$ which is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "c20572b7-1e75-5be2-a39c-b2327caff234" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5043 :tutorial/title "Properties of Symmetric Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "a119eca9-e695-53d9-9049-eef3212a6e22" :step/math-academy-id 31545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162925}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161816 :question/id #uuid "b159d52a-a436-5743-9151-2e147364a41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366739}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "dd82fa90-8f6a-5c31-a6bd-faa30ff2ee22" :step/math-academy-id 31546}] :activity/title "Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162214}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425156} :activity/id #uuid "4147fa8b-5ff8-579d-afae-acd5982a7b2d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/id #uuid "268943d4-aaa4-5e39-9db5-48d7523acdc3" :topic/math-academy-id 3118} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "345e70f6-3b7f-5da0-bde7-5d6dee2d58b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358217} {:answer/id #uuid "3826a4d7-56d7-5e10-a722-00186a58d1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358247} {:answer/id #uuid "a417c4ea-e08a-5c5c-9298-aba87be43f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359178} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e30533-75d2-5c1b-95fd-97a38042507a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359200} {:answer/id #uuid "d543ae34-7a48-5fe8-abcf-7b864c0a60f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359178} :answer-field/id #uuid "f979226a-2f92-55ff-a0a9-14997d804f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6c182c5-819c-5206-a031-9309a3557f8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96589" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n\nIII. If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "13d711fe-4f91-5fa4-8a59-b8df54fc9061" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aed7340-5f0e-5165-ab13-314353711b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355333} {:answer/id #uuid "8963ef11-348f-5e5f-8b9b-9f406cee908f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355880} {:answer/id #uuid "97fba95d-9f14-57b0-a978-2376e948fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356004} {:answer/id #uuid "bf821222-3076-59f2-8a78-9a7dfb62f4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356340} {:answer/id #uuid "e046d0c3-8ff2-5bd0-8adf-c6c8f1901ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355880} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67b1e1e-56ae-5451-a8ba-26dd0254b3a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "225f8bdf-5da4-5137-973e-d6466e5f7f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93985" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""} :step/id #uuid "850fd871-11f1-5a64-9c68-516ffbeed054" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa99686-720d-5c83-8792-909de2767703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425148} {:answer/id #uuid "13edd389-e8cd-52c8-8f6e-2c34ed7ad05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425149} {:answer/id #uuid "33ef6a26-6c4e-56d3-8e19-d9dc460624f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425150} {:answer/id #uuid "655424c1-b811-5aaf-8538-b1da76023605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425151} {:answer/id #uuid "65dc2a15-12f3-5c0d-999e-1bc8c09548b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425150} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dcd0455-6677-5df3-b08a-01a2a95723e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea940e2f-7cf1-5b69-9241-9cde37e5f341" :question/math-academy-id "q-140503" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ and $R$ only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ae65fa76-f15c-5a90-8603-42143fe53ed6"}] :activity/title "Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425147}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088797} :activity/id #uuid "22d6d34d-749f-599b-a696-f6952b1b29b5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608 :topic/id #uuid "5824a36d-203d-5834-aa63-4aceedf31f46" :topic/math-academy-id 906} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088944 :tutorial/content "A **first-order linear ordinary differential equation** is a differential equation in the variable $y(x)$ that can be expressed in the **general form**\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $f(x)$ are functions of $x$ only, and\n\n- $a(x)$ is not identically zero (i.e., it is not zero for all $x$).\n\nFor example, the differential equation\n\n$$\nxy' + e^x y=1\n$$\n\nis a first-order linear ODE with $a(x) = x, b(x) = e^x,$ and $f(x) = 1.$\n\nOn the other hand, the differential equation \n\n$$\nx(y')^2 +  e^x \\sin(y)=1\n$$\n\nis *not* a first-order linear ODE. Notice that it contains the terms $(y')^2$ and $\\sin(y),$ and therefore cannot be presented in the general form." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc4983db-1d7c-5573-a6d1-9e5e4a09207e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4055 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6cc3efa3-9929-5b62-9be5-629d74b8499c" :step/math-academy-id 593007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088945 :tutorial/content "The general form of a first-order linear ODE is\n\n$$\na(x)y'+b(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, if we take the general form and divide both sides by $a(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a(x)}{a(x)}{y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)} \\\\ {y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $a(x)$ is not identically zero, so we're allowed to perform this division.\n\nNow, if we define the functions $P$ and $Q$ as \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}, \\qquad Q(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{a(x)},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we can write our equation in the following **standard form**:\n\n$$\ny'+P(x)y =Q(x)\n$$\n\nFor example, given the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\ny' \\sin x+ y e^x= \\cos x,\n$$\n\nthe standard form of this equation can be found by dividing through by $\\sin x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+y(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{sin}x}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\ {y}^{'}+y{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{cot}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nP(x) = e^x \\csc x, \\qquad Q(x) = \\cot x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d0517ea0-b60e-5c34-bfbc-41fd6c61af75" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4056 :tutorial/title "The Standard Form of a First-Order Linear ODE"} :step/id #uuid "95fd0f61-4e3f-5214-a406-a8309198f92d" :step/math-academy-id 593008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087599 :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404259}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "7e7cbc14-094f-5a40-a91a-a3b62a3ad64b" :step/math-academy-id 593009 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087600 :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397780}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "29f2610f-f30f-5b70-bb79-098ffb891c67" :step/math-academy-id 593012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088862 :tutorial/content "Recall that the standard form of a first-order linear differential equation is\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that $Q(x)$ is called the **forcing function.**\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **homogeneous** if the forcing function $Q(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ In other words, the equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **inhomogeneous** if it is not homogeneous.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 0.\n$$\n\nThe equation matches the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = -x.$ Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Now, consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 6.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $Q(x) = 6$ means that the equation cannot be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is *not* homogeneous (i.e., it is *inhomogeneous*).\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + xy = 2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left-hand side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+xy & =2y \\\\ {y}^{'}+xy-2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{'}+(x-2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = (x-2).$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous." :tutorial/id #uuid "2107c0cc-b904-547f-b13d-9a0898b33d73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14653 :tutorial/title "Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Equations"} :step/id #uuid "b0d57580-3bcd-5a38-92ff-b1a7d2279815" :step/math-academy-id 593016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088802}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304943}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "685e843b-9372-5936-b5c3-cc142d209a6c" :step/math-academy-id 593017}] :activity/title "Introduction to First-Order Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087962}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120563} :activity/id #uuid "76251565-5039-56a6-8121-2814efe41dc1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050685 :topic/id #uuid "5ee1f34c-598f-5608-8eb7-1e1ffa254cd0" :topic/math-academy-id 1794} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120996 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) that's defined parametrically by \n![](images/a7/a74636de2b4b91905353ed8fd16c8af010260757f9cc70df2a24c23aef2ca55e.svg)\n\nThe point ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) corresponding to ![](images/40/407ab78b0b33045b1f97fec83d3d172fdbd3ff8eeebf622e2bb2b550e7bb498e.svg) on the curve has coordinates ![](images/51/51c3c69238e5ffe9c22e9a9a50dd89f69899db2c0895c5f39e2d80db74c6b3db.svg) We know that a tangent vector to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) is given by ![](images/b9/b9445cca9d656f361fdd089d2a38028714c21f8c5379a43731706bea0be49611.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2b4f4dad09042b761fe950c694c8cf645501106cd1d1d9c0f7a78967b224951c.png)\n\nWe compute ![](images/8a/8a5ec088ee8b89b6df1427fe455f10b0fc27842bf9bc4379d6b112daf01ec056.svg) as follows:\n![](images/88/8875ee3591bf9c6ccf0b3df9ca261c38894a134bb8229253145d000e6f6213fd.svg)\n\nSubstituting ![](images/40/407ab78b0b33045b1f97fec83d3d172fdbd3ff8eeebf622e2bb2b550e7bb498e.svg) into the above gives a tangent vector to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at ![](images/ed/ed03dfb662276f1766638162385f0cbef419ba1dd3a0ad02c9ee8d8a5d245cc9.svg)\n\n![](images/39/39ccc671a0f1a1b7df98a151741b7e9e46c0ee9cd8bfd858abe9a9ab4372f3d2.svg)\n\nAs we'll see, it's more helpful to work with unit vectors. Notice that ![](images/79/79a59608940ed4a5091b6b372dfd9bbdd186ab6f8afd946be2405dfd93e2b16d.svg) is not a unit vector because\n\n![](images/ae/ae44ee346812a97fc1e8935852d8615a4185458e1445cb39523d3003e26971f8.svg)\n\nHowever, we can create a unit vector, which we'll call ![](images/43/4326a76eb09b3a7888d11df0bb211ef28a4ab22bfd49a19ddcb96c9f9c98067e.svg) by dividing ![](images/79/79a59608940ed4a5091b6b372dfd9bbdd186ab6f8afd946be2405dfd93e2b16d.svg) by its magnitude:\n\n![](images/06/06ecc34ba9087997ec134c79f93d27ea2a0a3895c790ced864111add41ddc311.svg)\n\nIn general, for a vector valued function ![](images/e5/e5ced9a46fee369462905aace2537d978fd1222337b518b7fb49295793c28964.svg) the **unit tangent vector** ![](images/6f/6fd8da4e323d14ce0567026cff08b94311018226dcbd13f96777b640bb281fe9.svg) to the curve traced by ![](images/a0/a06debad6e153cb789c38ba57ea6e2ac7be55483b45b53a576dbbfa19715f637.svg) can be computed using the following formula:\n![](images/eb/eb59ef8c4faab00d31288eb311ed07c7787758c71d1e959ae7eabf198d7019a2.svg)\n\n**Note:** When we take a vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and create a unit vector that's parallel to it, we say that we have **normalized** the vector ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "de55871f-089d-5982-98d6-3ea78f7e3b5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3a2275fb-e73e-5073-a025-2bc601b975e5" :step/math-academy-id 10219 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119553 :question/id #uuid "c44c0973-5253-50c5-98b1-15938b79494e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391617}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "b35664ff-b09b-5220-918b-2c8fac890599" :step/math-academy-id 10220 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119554 :question/id #uuid "95ba9167-9e49-557d-bfbd-89bdeec8939f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3055"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c400d85b-94d5-5fd2-98b4-472ab43c5be4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049360}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387789} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218639344